<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Debug builds only (a `flutter run` from a Mac, which nobody can do yet).
	     Sandbox APNs, because a development-signed build gets sandbox tokens.
	     Release/Profile use Runner.entitlements. -->
	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>development</string>

	<!-- Tap to Pay on iPhone. Present HERE and deliberately NOT in
	     Runner.entitlements, because the two entitlements Apple grants are
	     separate and arrive at different times:

	       * the DEVELOPMENT entitlement (granted 2026-07-31) enables the
	         capability on the App ID for *development* provisioning profiles
	         only — which is exactly what a Debug build signs with, so a real
	         charge on a tethered iPhone works with this key.
	       * the PUBLISHING entitlement — the one App Review grants after the
	         checklist in TAPTOPAY.md — is what makes a *distribution* profile
	         carry it. Until that lands, putting this key in
	         Runner.entitlements means cloud signing can't build a profile that
	         satisfies it and every Release/TestFlight export fails.

	     So: add it to Runner.entitlements the day the publishing grant email
	     arrives, not before. Note that none of the merchant-education UI needs
	     this at all — ProximityReaderDiscovery presents education, not a
	     reader, so that half is testable in any build. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.proximity-reader.payment.acceptance</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Sign in with Apple — see the note in Runner.entitlements. Present in
	     both files because, unlike Tap to Pay, this capability is self-serve on
	     the App ID and applies to development and distribution profiles alike;
	     omitting it here would make the button fail only in debug builds, which
	     is precisely where it gets tested. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.applesignin</key>
	<array>
		<string>Default</string>
	</array>

	<!-- Square's Mobile Payments SDK requires THREE capabilities on iOS, and the
	     app shipped with only the Tap to Pay one for weeks. From Square's own
	     iOS setup: "add these capabilities: App Attest, NFC Tag Reading, Tap to
	     Pay on iPhone", then regenerate the provisioning profile.

	     **App Attest is the mechanism behind the application signature.** Square
	     requires every integration on SDK 2.1+ to register a bundle id + team id
	     in the Developer Console before it will take production payments — "an
	     extra layer of security to ensure that your application hasn't been
	     tampered with" — and App Attest is how the app proves it is that
	     application. Both halves are required and neither says so when it
	     fails: with the signature unregistered, or with it registered but no
	     way to attest, Square declines to arm the reader and shows its generic
	     "connect hardware to take card payments" screen. There is no error code
	     for it in the SDK (see its documented error enums), nothing in the
	     device log from Square, and the failure is indistinguishable from
	     missing hardware or a missing entitlement. That cost several debugging
	     sessions on 2026-09-02, on a phone whose Apple-side reader session was
	     being created correctly the whole time.

	     `production`, not `development`, and deliberately so even here in the
	     debug/development entitlements: these builds take **real** payments
	     against the production Square application, and Square validates the
	     attestation server-side against Apple's production App Attest root. A
	     development attestation carries a different root and would be rejected.
	     If Square ever refuses this one, its note that "debug builds taking
	     production payments also require separate signatures" is the thread to
	     pull, not this value.

	     Unlike Tap to Pay, both of these are **self-serve** on the App ID
	     (Certificates, Identifiers & Profiles) — no Apple review, no waiting.
	     Enabling them invalidates existing provisioning profiles, which costs
	     nothing here: both release workflows sign with `-allowProvisioningUpdates`
	     and cloud signing regenerates the profile on the next run. But the App ID
	     must have the capabilities BEFORE a build carries these keys, or signing
	     fails outright — the same ordering trap as the Tap to Pay key above. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.devicecheck.appattest-environment</key>
	<string>production</string>
	<key>com.apple.developer.nfc.readersession.formats</key>
	<array>
		<string>NDEF</string>
		<string>TAG</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
